    "value": "9"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2018-p7.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(4000 = 2^5 \\cdot 5^3,\\) the expression equals \\(2^{5+n} \\cdot 5^{3-n}.\\) This is an integer exactly when both \\(5 + n \\ge 0\\) and \\(3 - n \\ge 0,\\) that is, \\(-5 \\le n \\le 3.\\) There are \\(3 - (-5) + 1 = 9\\) such integers.\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Write \\(4000 = 2^5 \\cdot 5^3,\\) so the expression is \\(2^{5+n} \\cdot 5^{3-n}\\)",
        "This is an integer exactly when both exponents \\(5 + n\\) and \\(3 - n\\) are nonnegative"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
